  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMorning water vapor imagery shows the upper level trough beginning\nto move across the California Bight with a weak Atmospheric River\nfeeding into far southern CA. Widespread light rainfall continues\nthis morning with rainfall rates generally 0.10 inch/hour or less.\nIsolated heavier showers with rates near 0.30-0.50 inch/hour are \npossible later this morning as colder air aloft moves overhead.\nRain will transition to more scattered showers around sunrise,\nthen taper off from north to south this afternoon, ending by late\nevening. \n\nAdditional rainfall totals will range anywhere from 0.25-0.75\" \nfor the coasts to the mountains, locally 1\" or more in the San \nDiego County Mountains, and 0.05-0.30\" in the deserts, highest in \nSan Diego County. Snow levels near 9500-11000 ft this morning will\ngradually fall to 7000-7500 ft this evening as precipitation \nends, with only a light dusting on the highest peaks of the San \nBernardino and San Jacinto Mountains. Otherwise cool today with\nhigh temperatures 10-15 degrees below normal.\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure moves in from the west on\nMonday and Tuesday. This ridge, aided by weak offshore flow, will\nprovide warmer and drier weather Monday and Tuesday, though highs\nwill still be a few degrees below normal - generally low to mid\n70s across the coastal basin, mid 60s to mid 60s in the mountains,\nupper 60s to low 70s in the high desert, and upper 70s to low 80s\nin the low desert. \n\nAnother upper trough moves through California late Tuesday into\nWednesday morning, but at this time chances of measurable \nprecipitation are less than 10%. Another round of weak offshore\nflow follows for the remainder of the day Wednesday into Thursday\nunder weakly cyclonic flow, bringing minor warming. Ensembles\nbegin to diverge a bit more Friday and Saturday with the potential\ndevelopment of another low digging into the Desert Southwest.\nAround 23% of the ensemble members have a deeper low for cooler\nweather but slightly stronger offshore flow, while the remaining\nmembers have a much weaker trough Friday and ridging by Saturday\nfor weaker offshore flow and warmer weather.",
